+\paragraph{Adversary and access.}\label{sec:threat} A \emph{recipient} is a party permitted one purpose. In our
+evaluation, for each scored record it receives that purpose's release under the contract in force, and nothing else
+about the record: not its inputs, its true task label or its protected attribute, and not the model's parameters,
+gradients or internal states (defense maps, heads, or a representation that the contract does not release). Each
+record is released once, and no attacker submits inputs of its choosing. Our attackers are supervised: each learns to
+turn a release into a guess about the protected attribute from labelled auxiliary data that the benchmark grants,
+namely the releases of the records in \texttt{attacker\_fit} together with their protected attribute, and a second
+labelled set, \texttt{attacker\_val}, on which the attacker is chosen and oriented. Both come from the same population
+as the scored records, and these two roles (Section~\ref{sec:protocol}) fix the attacker's data budget. This grant is
+an assumption of the benchmark. It models a recipient that knows the attribute for some of the people it serves, as in
+attribute-inference work in which attackers train on labelled examples or on people who disclosed the attribute
+\citep{song2020overlearning,jia2018attriguard}; we do not show that a recipient can obtain such data, in this amount
+or from the same population, in practice. Attacks that need no such sample, for example ones that use a known
+population law, are not evaluated. A \emph{coalition} of two recipients holds both recipients' releases for the same
+record, perfectly linked. We do not assume that contracts or governance prevent such linkage; perfect linkage is the
+attacker-favourable limit, and we model no linkage error. Attackers are refitted to the releases of the defended
+system and so adapt to the distribution that the defense emits; only in that limited sense are they defense-aware. A
+recipient may know a defense's published algorithm and objective, but our attackers use no such knowledge beyond this
+refitting, and none uses fitted parameters or gradients. Record and household identifiers assign roles and align
+releases; they are never attacker inputs. This scope isolates one narrow question: what attackers that learn from
+this labelled sample recover from the releases alone. A record's own inputs in the attacker's hands, public model
+parameters or gradients, permitted repeated or chosen queries, attacks that exploit a defense's published objective or
+its parameters, and insiders each define a different threat model (Section~\ref{sec:limits}); we do not test them, and
+we neither claim protection against them nor dismiss them. Recovery is therefore a measured property of a fixed
+attacker slate under this conditional threat model, not a privacy guarantee.
